## Contract
Linear search traverses an array sequentially until it finds a target value, returning its index (0-based) or -1 if not found. Time complexity: O(n). Space complexity: O(1).

## Dependencies
- `array-base`: Array parameter conventions (ptr + length)
- `comparison`: `compare_eq(a, b)` for equality check

## Prohibitions
- `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`, `free` — no dynamic allocation

## Slots
| Slot | Type | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| `arr[]` | `int[]` | Input array to search |
| `n` | `int` | Number of elements in array |
| `target` | `int` | Value to find |

## Boundaries
- Empty array (`n = 0`): returns -1 without entering loop
- Target at index 0: returns 0 on first iteration
- Target at last index: iterates through all n-1 elements
- Target not present: completes full traversal, returns -1
- Duplicate values: returns first occurrence (lowest index)

## Examples
```c
// Found at index 2
int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};
int idx = linear_search(arr, 4, 30);  // → 2

// Not found
int idx = linear_search(arr, 4, 99);   // → -1

// Empty array
int idx = linear_search(arr, 0, 10);   // → -1
```

[code]
```c
// Linear search — O(n), returns index or -1 if not found
int linear_search(int arr[], int n, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (compare_eq(arr[i], target)) {
            return i;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```
